# Design mockups are a specification, not an inspiration

<!-- core -->
Build it exactly as drawn: layout, spacing, sizes, colours, typography, radii, shadows, icons, and
the literal copy — typos included. **No unrequested improvements** — if the mockup looks wrong, say
so and build it as drawn unless the user agrees to change it. Reuse existing tokens where they
already match; add new ones where the mockup demands values the system lacks, rather than bending
the mockup to fit.

**Ask rather than invent.** Mockups always underspecify — interaction, hover/focus/disabled,
loading/empty/error, validation, breakpoints, overflow. Batch the questions up front; ask mid-build
the moment a real blocker appears. Verify visually before claiming it matches: run it, screenshot
it, compare side by side.

<!-- /core -->

Applies to any mockup the user presents — an image, a Figma frame or link, a screenshot, an HTML
prototype, a PDF.

## Match it

Layout, spacing, sizes, colours, typography (family, weight, size, line height, letter spacing),
border radii, shadows, icons, and the literal copy shown. Reproduce the mockup's text verbatim,
typos included, unless told otherwise.

## No unrequested improvements

Do not "fix" alignment, rebalance whitespace, substitute a different component, modernise the
styling, or swap the palette because something looks off.

The instinct to improve is the failure mode here. A mockup that looks slightly wrong is usually
either deliberate or already agreed with someone you are not talking to.

## Ask rather than invent

Mockups are static and always underspecify. The moment something the mockup does not answer comes
up, ask — do not guess, and do not silently pick a default. Typical gaps:

- Behaviour of interactive elements.
- Hover, focus, active and disabled states.
- Loading, empty, error and overflow states.
- Validation rules and messages.
- Responsive behaviour at breakpoints the mockup does not show.
- What happens with long text or large numbers.
- Exact assets or icon sets.
- Which real data feeds which element.

Where a gap is small and clearly answered by an established convention already in
the codebase, follow that convention and say so.

## Verify visually before claiming it is done

Run the app, screenshot the page, compare it against the mockup side by side. "It should match" is
not verification.

Applies globally, all projects, no exceptions.
